1
00:00:00,001 --> 00:00:06,460
It arcs, explodes, and blisters steel.

2
00:00:07,160 --> 00:00:09,740
It's used to make 50% of all products,

3
00:00:09,940 --> 00:00:13,600
and it puts the power to build a skyscraper in a man's hands.

4
00:00:14,380 --> 00:00:17,580
By friction or robot, even underwater,

5
00:00:18,420 --> 00:00:21,180
these are the tools that the world can't live without.

6
00:00:22,100 --> 00:00:24,960
Now, welding on Modern Marbles.

7
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:40,860
Las Vegas, Nevada.

8
00:00:42,320 --> 00:00:46,540
Perhaps nowhere else in the world does the old come down as spectacularly.

9
00:00:51,960 --> 00:00:53,880
And the new rise up as quickly.

10
00:00:54,820 --> 00:00:58,900
Right now you're in the east wing, the new Plaza Hotel.

11
00:00:58,901 --> 00:01:00,140
We're on the 38th floor.

12
00:01:01,620 --> 00:01:02,920
There we go, laying deck.

13
00:01:04,600 --> 00:01:10,700
The Palazzo Hotel will be the central hub of the world's largest resort, hotel, and casino complex.

14
00:01:11,120 --> 00:01:14,000
And without the power of welding, it couldn't be built.

15
00:01:15,180 --> 00:01:17,180
Armed with high-power welding guns,

16
00:01:18,120 --> 00:01:21,800
and with nothing more than a safety harness between them and the ground below,

17
00:01:22,580 --> 00:01:27,720
these ironworkers will weld a quantity of steel nearly equal to the amount in the Brooklyn Bridge,

18
00:01:27,721 --> 00:01:30,820
Statue of Liberty and Empire State Building combined.

19
00:01:32,020 --> 00:01:34,900
There's just shy of 70,000 tons.

20
00:01:38,280 --> 00:01:41,240
Adrenaline runs high for welders working at such heights.

21
00:01:43,880 --> 00:01:46,120
And with so much electricity in use,

22
00:01:47,260 --> 00:01:48,940
the dangers are real.

23
00:01:50,120 --> 00:01:54,060
This particular machine runs on 483 phase.

24
00:01:54,180 --> 00:01:56,600
It's fed with 200 amps at 480.

25
00:01:56,601 --> 00:01:58,760
If you make a mistake, you're not here tomorrow.

26
00:01:59,080 --> 00:02:00,680
It's almost instantaneous death.

27
00:02:04,180 --> 00:02:09,320
By definition, welding joins two separate pieces of material through high energy.

28
00:02:10,180 --> 00:02:13,760
No other joining method forms a more direct and powerful bond.

29
00:02:14,780 --> 00:02:21,880
All the other methods, whether it's rivets, whether it's bolts, even if it's glues and adhesives,

30
00:02:22,260 --> 00:02:25,780
you wind up putting in extra material in order to make the connection.

31
00:02:25,781 --> 00:02:31,780
In the case of welding connections, bring the two pieces together, put a weld in between.

32
00:02:31,781 --> 00:02:35,780
It's a very efficient way of joining material.

33
00:02:35,781 --> 00:02:38,780
But efficiency is only half the story.

34
00:02:38,781 --> 00:02:45,780
Because welding creates a bond along a seam or joint that is nearly always stronger than the base metals used to form it.

35
00:02:45,781 --> 00:02:50,780
The tensile strength of the welded material itself is actually greater than the material you're welding.

36
00:02:50,781 --> 00:02:54,780
A good weld, the material itself will tear before the weld will tear.

37
00:02:55,780 --> 00:03:01,920
The source of this strength is more than just surface deep, and goes right down to the steel's very atoms.

38
00:03:03,220 --> 00:03:08,900
Every atom within a beam possesses electrons that encircle it in what is called an electron cloud.

39
00:03:09,860 --> 00:03:18,100
When a welder applies intense heat, the atoms are slammed together so forcefully that they begin to share a single, united electron cloud.

40
00:03:19,540 --> 00:03:24,320
Now locked as one, these new molecules are more powerful together than separate.

41
00:03:25,780 --> 00:03:32,780
For many centuries, the only way to make these welded bonds was with furnace and hammer,

42
00:03:32,781 --> 00:03:34,780
a process now known as forge welding.

43
00:03:34,781 --> 00:03:41,780
With forge welding, two pieces of material are put, lapped over each other, heated, not

44
00:03:41,781 --> 00:03:45,780
to the point of melting, but until they're hot, and then hammered together.

45
00:03:45,781 --> 00:03:50,780
That hammering together the two metals will achieve the same metallic bonds that today

46
00:03:50,781 --> 00:03:53,780
we'd form with an electric car.

47
00:03:53,781 --> 00:03:59,780
Today, electricity has replaced the brute force of the forger's hammer to construct our world.

48
00:03:59,781 --> 00:04:05,780
From the car you drive, to the plane you fly, and everywhere in between.

49
00:04:05,781 --> 00:04:08,780
The most common method is known as electric arc welding,

50
00:04:08,781 --> 00:04:12,780
which is based on principles first discovered at the turn of the 19th century.

51
00:04:14,780 --> 00:04:20,780
Those principles revealed that electric current will jump the gap between two nearby metal conductors

52
00:04:20,781 --> 00:04:23,780
to form a completed electrical circuit.

53
00:04:25,780 --> 00:04:27,780
This jump, known as an electric arc,

54
00:04:27,781 --> 00:04:31,780
generates a spark-like discharge that is both extremely bright

55
00:04:31,781 --> 00:04:33,780
and intensely hot.

56
00:04:33,781 --> 00:04:40,780
The arc is estimated to be between 6,000 and 8,000 degrees Fahrenheit.

57
00:04:40,781 --> 00:04:42,780
That's the same temperature as the sun.

58
00:04:42,781 --> 00:04:47,780
Steel melts in and around 3,000 degrees,

59
00:04:47,781 --> 00:04:54,240
So this 6 to 8,000 degree arc is more than ample heat to melt the steel that we join in.

60
00:04:55,360 --> 00:05:02,660
By the 1890s, engineers had harnessed this intense newfound energy to create the first electric arc welds.

61
00:05:04,400 --> 00:05:08,620
A work lead, or ground, was attached to one corner of the metal to be welded.

62
00:05:09,280 --> 00:05:14,580
Electric current then flowed from a generator through a long conductive metal stick, known as an electrode.

63
00:05:14,581 --> 00:05:21,540
As the stick neared the grounded metal, an electric arc was formed, causing heat to melt the metal.

64
00:05:22,560 --> 00:05:25,600
The apparatus was crude, but the basics were sound,

65
00:05:26,220 --> 00:05:30,960
and are now reflected in the simplest arc welding form in use today, stick welding.

66
00:05:31,880 --> 00:05:36,040
The basics of the process are, you have an electrode holder,

67
00:05:36,880 --> 00:05:41,400
you have a cable that runs back to the machine to pick up the welding current,

68
00:05:41,401 --> 00:05:44,380
and you place the bare end of the electrode in the holder,

69
00:05:44,580 --> 00:05:47,500
and it picks up electrical contact there,

70
00:05:48,240 --> 00:05:50,480
conducts current down to the end of the electrode, which is bare.

71
00:05:51,160 --> 00:05:54,100
The technique is touching the electrode against the work

72
00:05:54,101 --> 00:05:57,220
and then moving it far enough away to establish an arc,

73
00:05:57,320 --> 00:05:58,520
but not so far it goes out.

74
00:05:59,080 --> 00:06:00,800
And if you leave it too close, it'll stick.

75
00:06:01,460 --> 00:06:03,240
If you pull away too far, it'll go out.

76
00:06:03,241 --> 00:06:06,900
So the trick is to get your bounce where it's just about right.

77
00:06:08,080 --> 00:06:10,980
When the arc is struck and positioned near the metal pieces,

78
00:06:10,981 --> 00:06:15,480
the intense heat causes both the electrode and the metal along the seam

79
00:06:15,481 --> 00:06:18,920
to melt together into what is called the puddle.

80
00:06:20,080 --> 00:06:23,940
Until it hardens, this molten puddle must be protected,

81
00:06:24,680 --> 00:06:26,540
even from the very air that we breathe.

82
00:06:27,880 --> 00:06:34,060
The air we breathe is about 80% nitrogen and about 19% oxygen.

83
00:06:35,060 --> 00:06:37,980
It's really good for breathing, but it's not good for liquid metal.

84
00:06:37,981 --> 00:06:45,360
And just like we dissolve sugar in hot coffee, these gases dissolve in liquid metal.

85
00:06:45,780 --> 00:06:52,260
When the metal solidifies, the gas percolates out, and that makes holes in the well.

86
00:06:53,980 --> 00:06:57,480
The dangerous result could be a weak, even brittle, well.

87
00:06:57,940 --> 00:07:01,280
So to shield the puddle, a chemical coating is applied to the electrode.

88
00:07:01,281 --> 00:07:08,280
As it melts under the heat of the arc, this chemical coating dissolves into shielding

89
00:07:08,281 --> 00:07:13,960
gases that envelop and protect the weld, keeping it free of air.

90
00:07:13,961 --> 00:07:17,360
This is known as shielded metal arc welding.

91
00:07:17,361 --> 00:07:22,640
And as the technology evolved, the coating took on even greater significance.

92
00:07:22,641 --> 00:07:25,180
The chemical coating has three functions.

93
00:07:25,181 --> 00:07:28,900
Some of the coating forms shielding gases at the heat of the arc to protect the weld

94
00:07:28,901 --> 00:07:31,080
from the atmosphere.

95
00:07:31,081 --> 00:07:34,560
Some of the chemicals at the heat of that arc form a liquid, slag, which protects the

96
00:07:34,561 --> 00:07:37,500
welds as the shielding gases move along.

97
00:07:37,501 --> 00:07:41,180
And some of the elements are alloyed into the deposit to make it a high strength deposit.

98
00:07:41,181 --> 00:07:46,680
So three things happen while you're moving this arc along the work.

99
00:07:46,681 --> 00:07:51,460
When the weld is complete and is cooled, the slag is chipped away to reveal the trail of

100
00:07:51,461 --> 00:07:56,020
welded metal, known as the bead.

101
00:07:56,021 --> 00:08:00,080
the proper length, depth, and form of the puddle along the bead.

102
00:08:00,081 --> 00:08:03,080
Takes years of practice.

103
00:08:03,081 --> 00:08:05,080
There's so many variables when you weld.

104
00:08:05,081 --> 00:08:08,160
There's very few times that two things you weld together

105
00:08:08,161 --> 00:08:09,160
are the same.

106
00:08:09,161 --> 00:08:12,060
Either the metal changes, the thickness of metal,

107
00:08:12,061 --> 00:08:14,540
the environment you're in, the conditions

108
00:08:14,541 --> 00:08:16,300
the metal is used for.

109
00:08:16,301 --> 00:08:18,080
There are just so many things changed

110
00:08:18,081 --> 00:08:22,040
that you have to know a lot of different things about welding.

111
00:08:22,041 --> 00:08:24,840
Complicating matters are the safety requirements,

112
00:08:24,841 --> 00:08:26,860
beginning with a welder's mask.

113
00:08:26,861 --> 00:08:29,580
Just as staring into the sun can damage your eyes,

114
00:08:29,581 --> 00:08:32,100
so too can an electric arc.

115
00:08:32,101 --> 00:08:34,540
Therefore, protection is crucial.

116
00:08:34,541 --> 00:08:36,120
When you first flip it down, it's dark.

117
00:08:36,121 --> 00:08:37,120
You can't see anything,

118
00:08:37,121 --> 00:08:39,080
but you quickly get comfortable with it.

119
00:08:39,081 --> 00:08:41,560
Once you strike the arc, it's like a big flashlight.

120
00:08:41,561 --> 00:08:43,060
You can see what you're doing.

121
00:08:45,440 --> 00:08:46,760
Although electric arc welding

122
00:08:46,761 --> 00:08:49,080
was first discovered in the 1800s,

123
00:08:49,081 --> 00:08:50,880
its acceptance was slow in coming.

124
00:08:52,120 --> 00:08:53,840
Anything new, they fight.

125
00:08:53,841 --> 00:08:59,280
It wasn't thoroughly accepted, so it took time to make the transition into the welding process.

126
00:09:01,120 --> 00:09:06,440
No company was more influential in changing the tide than Lincoln Electric Company of Cleveland, Ohio.

127
00:09:08,800 --> 00:09:14,180
Today, Lincoln Electric is the largest manufacturer of welding equipment and consumables in the world.

128
00:09:15,960 --> 00:09:20,120
That success is rooted in Lincoln's commitment to proving the viability of welding,

129
00:09:20,680 --> 00:09:22,880
even in the face of constant doubters.

130
00:09:23,840 --> 00:09:31,460
And no industry was initially more skeptical than the biggest one of all, building construction,

131
00:09:31,461 --> 00:09:34,840
where rivets were king well into the 1940s.

132
00:09:34,841 --> 00:09:37,880
Rivets were intuitive.

133
00:09:37,881 --> 00:09:43,600
If you think about it, putting in something with a head on either side, that makes sense.

134
00:09:43,601 --> 00:09:46,720
Welding had some mystery about it.

135
00:09:46,721 --> 00:09:50,220
But riveting was also extremely difficult work.

136
00:09:50,221 --> 00:09:54,120
required fabricators to hole punch the steel beams.

137
00:09:54,121 --> 00:09:56,200
Then a team of workers aligned those holes

138
00:09:56,201 --> 00:09:58,340
to their exact counterpart.

139
00:09:58,341 --> 00:10:00,720
Rivets were heated in a central oven,

140
00:10:00,721 --> 00:10:02,820
then hurled to the proper joint.

141
00:10:02,821 --> 00:10:04,140
He would heat the rivets.

142
00:10:04,141 --> 00:10:05,980
And once they got heated, he would

143
00:10:05,981 --> 00:10:08,820
throw them to the person who was actually standing

144
00:10:08,821 --> 00:10:11,060
at the point waiting for the first rivet.

145
00:10:11,061 --> 00:10:14,480
So you had a pitcher, a catcher, with a set of tongs

146
00:10:14,481 --> 00:10:16,380
who would slide it into the hole.

147
00:10:16,381 --> 00:10:19,280
And then you had a guy bucket, and then you had a riveter.

148
00:10:19,281 --> 00:10:22,040
and the river would round the head on the opposite side.

149
00:10:23,280 --> 00:10:26,440
Significantly, rivets limited the design options

150
00:10:26,441 --> 00:10:28,400
for architects and building engineers.

151
00:10:29,600 --> 00:10:32,720
A lot of very famous and beautiful work

152
00:10:32,721 --> 00:10:34,140
is done with rivets.

153
00:10:34,141 --> 00:10:36,040
But basically you're building a box

154
00:10:36,041 --> 00:10:38,440
with a design to the inside of the box.

155
00:10:38,441 --> 00:10:40,960
You can only carry so much shear on that connection.

156
00:10:42,220 --> 00:10:45,180
In 1928, Lincoln Electric joined

157
00:10:45,181 --> 00:10:46,960
with a local architectural firm

158
00:10:46,961 --> 00:10:48,660
to erect the first commercial building

159
00:10:48,661 --> 00:10:52,560
wholly constructed from arc welded steel.

160
00:10:52,561 --> 00:10:57,940
In the upper Carnegie building, the typical dense network of riveted steel was replaced

161
00:10:57,941 --> 00:11:04,480
by a series of continuously welded beams that ran the entire length and height of the building.

162
00:11:04,481 --> 00:11:09,380
Not only did welding free up space, but these continuous beams carried even greater loads

163
00:11:09,381 --> 00:11:12,600
and stresses than their riveted counterparts.

164
00:11:12,601 --> 00:11:17,600
Connections are always the weak point in the structure.

165
00:11:17,601 --> 00:11:21,600
Connecting things together is always a challenge.

166
00:11:21,601 --> 00:11:23,600
Welding changed all that.

167
00:11:23,601 --> 00:11:27,600
Literally, if you could get the material in the configuration you wanted,

168
00:11:27,601 --> 00:11:29,600
there was a way to weld it together.

169
00:11:29,601 --> 00:11:33,600
Today, skyscrapers come in many shapes and sizes,

170
00:11:33,601 --> 00:11:37,600
in large part because continuous beams can be fabricated and welded

171
00:11:37,601 --> 00:11:40,600
into curves and unique angles.

172
00:11:40,601 --> 00:11:50,600
To construct these beams, welders will make 30 to 40 passes on the crucial joints, using Lincoln Electric's latest flux core welding technology.

173
00:11:51,600 --> 00:11:59,600
Here, the traditional stick electrode has been replaced by wire on a roll that feeds directly into the welder's gun.

174
00:12:00,600 --> 00:12:07,600
The chemical flux that protects the weld from the air is now contained within the wire itself and melts just as before.

175
00:12:07,601 --> 00:12:14,600
There's approximately 90 tons of weld wire will be used and consumed in this project alone.

176
00:12:14,601 --> 00:12:21,600
That's somewhere around 180 million inches or 2,800 miles of weld wire.

177
00:12:21,601 --> 00:12:26,600
You could stretch a single wire from L.A. to New York and have leftover.

178
00:12:26,601 --> 00:12:31,600
On the largest beams, which can weigh as much as 900 pounds per linear foot,

179
00:12:31,601 --> 00:12:36,600
a team of welders can work on a single weld for hours.

180
00:12:36,601 --> 00:12:41,960
The columns that we're looking at right now are basically supporting the entire east wing of this tower.

181
00:12:42,480 --> 00:12:47,720
Originally, these pieces were so long, the capacity of the cranes would not pick them up and set them.

182
00:12:48,740 --> 00:12:52,520
So we had to cut them down, make them lighter, so the crane could set them.

183
00:12:53,600 --> 00:12:56,760
And the engineers wouldn't allow us to make that a bolted splice.

184
00:12:56,840 --> 00:12:58,280
It had to become a welded splice.

185
00:12:58,720 --> 00:13:00,380
It's about a 30-hour weld.

186
00:13:00,480 --> 00:13:04,840
I believe we had two guys working on that simultaneously for 30 hours of welding.

187
00:13:06,600 --> 00:13:15,360
Welding really enabled the architect, the engineer to bring bigger dreams, make bigger

188
00:13:15,361 --> 00:13:21,500
bridges, make taller skyscrapers, and to do so in a reliable and dependable way.

189
00:13:21,501 --> 00:13:28,120
But at the end of the day, those big dreams are only as solid as the iron workers who

190
00:13:28,121 --> 00:13:29,120
weld them together.

191
00:13:29,121 --> 00:13:30,120
We're not presidents.

192
00:13:30,121 --> 00:13:32,580
We don't get monuments built to us.

193
00:13:32,581 --> 00:13:37,140
But when you look across the skyline, iron workers have changed the way that looks.

194
00:13:37,141 --> 00:13:41,640
But electric arcs aren't the only way to make a weld.

195
00:13:41,641 --> 00:13:48,360
In fact, others are a real blast.

196
00:13:48,361 --> 00:13:54,880
In December 2006, 27 megasteel columns were arc welded together to anchor New York's Freedom

197
00:13:54,881 --> 00:13:58,320
Tower at the former site of the World Trade Center.

198
00:13:58,321 --> 00:14:04,240
Each column stands more than five stories tall and weighs as much as 30 tons.

199
00:14:04,241 --> 00:14:09,440
Welding will return on Modern Marvels.

200
00:14:09,441 --> 00:14:14,560
We now return to welding on Modern Marvels.

201
00:14:14,561 --> 00:14:17,840
Pennsylvania's Allegheny Mountains.

202
00:14:17,841 --> 00:14:21,320
Birthplace of American steel.

203
00:14:21,321 --> 00:14:26,140
Here in the hillsides that surround historic mill towns, the most powerful welding process

204
00:14:26,141 --> 00:14:27,840
of all occurs.

205
00:14:27,841 --> 00:14:29,360
than half a mile underground.

206
00:14:30,260 --> 00:14:31,120
Fire no!

207
00:14:36,380 --> 00:14:38,700
It's called explosion welding.

208
00:14:42,320 --> 00:14:45,760
And with a force measured in millions of pounds per square inch,

209
00:14:46,840 --> 00:14:49,580
explosion welding does what no other welding method can.

210
00:14:50,680 --> 00:14:52,900
Join nearly every kind of metal together,

211
00:14:53,160 --> 00:14:55,200
no matter the type or composition.

212
00:14:55,201 --> 00:15:02,640
explosion welding allows highly dissimilar metals such as aluminum carbon steel alloy

213
00:15:02,641 --> 00:15:10,960
steel stainless steels alloys of copper all can be welded to one another the result a single welded

214
00:15:10,961 --> 00:15:15,840
piece known as clad that combines the best characteristics of each metal involved

215
00:15:18,160 --> 00:15:24,880
wherever there is high heat intense pressure or corrosive liquids and gases clad is probably there

216
00:15:25,200 --> 00:15:30,120
It could be a column, it could be a heat exchanger, it could be a horizontal tank.

217
00:15:30,121 --> 00:15:35,680
But when you see a chemical complex or an oil refinery, there will be a lot of clad metal in there.

218
00:15:37,620 --> 00:15:42,400
To create an explosion weld, two large pieces of metal are stacked atop one another,

219
00:15:43,200 --> 00:15:46,700
then covered with a high-powered explosive.

220
00:15:46,701 --> 00:15:59,140
When detonated, the downward force of the explosion welds the two pieces together through

221
00:15:59,141 --> 00:16:02,300
a combination of intense force and remarkable physics.

222
00:16:02,301 --> 00:16:06,640
What you can see here, and it's very clear, you have two different materials.

223
00:16:07,020 --> 00:16:11,320
Stainless steel is a darker gray, a lighter gray for the carbon steel material.

224
00:16:11,321 --> 00:16:17,700
The detonation was initiated at this point, and you can see the deformation from our initiator.

225
00:16:18,060 --> 00:16:24,640
That starts the explosion, and the explosion rolls across the entire top surface of the plate, zipping the two together.

226
00:16:30,400 --> 00:16:34,680
No one would have ever thought such a violent process could be controlled and mastered

227
00:16:34,681 --> 00:16:40,880
had it not been for the devastation and havoc of World War I, and later World War II.

228
00:16:41,320 --> 00:16:47,080
The origin of explosion welding was first observed during the First World War,

229
00:16:47,380 --> 00:16:49,500
when shrapnel may have stuck to armament.

230
00:16:50,460 --> 00:16:52,780
It wasn't just stuck, but it was actually welded.

231
00:16:54,440 --> 00:16:56,960
There was only one possible explanation,

232
00:16:57,680 --> 00:17:00,160
the explosive force these metal pieces had endured.

233
00:17:00,980 --> 00:17:05,800
It was an observed phenomenon that was later duplicated in the laboratories

234
00:17:05,801 --> 00:17:07,900
and practiced commercially.

235
00:17:07,901 --> 00:17:14,900
Today, Dynamics Materials Corporation is a world leader in explosion welding technology.

236
00:17:14,901 --> 00:17:20,900
Here, the decades-old discoveries from the battlefield have been refined into an exacting science.

237
00:17:20,901 --> 00:17:30,900
The explosion welding process begins as soon as the two metal plates arrive at DMC's main plant, outside of Pittsburgh, Pennsylvania.

238
00:17:30,901 --> 00:17:38,900
To maximize the welding force of the explosion, the surfaces of each plate are ground as uniformly

239
00:17:38,901 --> 00:17:45,900
flat as possible, a process that also removes any rust, oxides, and other surface flaws.

240
00:17:45,901 --> 00:17:52,120
They are then ready to be assembled into the pack, which locks the plates into position

241
00:17:52,121 --> 00:17:54,780
for the explosion.

242
00:17:54,781 --> 00:17:58,960
To build a pack, the stronger and thicker of the two plates is laid face up.

243
00:17:58,961 --> 00:18:03,960
From now on this plate will be identified as the backer.

244
00:18:03,961 --> 00:18:08,280
Small metal spacers of equal height are then tacked onto the surface of the backer in a

245
00:18:08,281 --> 00:18:10,560
uniform grid.

246
00:18:10,561 --> 00:18:14,540
These spacers will maintain a set gap between the backer and the second plate, which is

247
00:18:14,541 --> 00:18:16,520
placed on top.

248
00:18:16,521 --> 00:18:21,960
This second plate is thinner than the backer and is called the clatter.

249
00:18:21,961 --> 00:18:27,200
The standoff gap between the backer and clatter is less than an inch in height.

250
00:18:27,201 --> 00:18:29,700
Without it, the explosion weld would be impossible.

251
00:18:31,480 --> 00:18:33,820
In the final stage of assembling the pack,

252
00:18:34,180 --> 00:18:37,420
a folding wooden frame is constructed along the edges of the clatter.

253
00:18:38,600 --> 00:18:42,620
When this frame is later unfolded inside the underground explosion chamber,

254
00:18:43,240 --> 00:18:47,740
it serves as the bed for the explosive powder that is poured on top.

255
00:18:48,960 --> 00:18:53,400
The three essential variables of an explosion weld are,

256
00:18:53,401 --> 00:19:02,760
First, the standoff gap, the spacing between the two metals needs to be very tightly controlled to ensure the highest quality weld.

257
00:19:03,200 --> 00:19:05,800
The second two parameters deal with the explosive.

258
00:19:06,240 --> 00:19:10,420
One is the velocity of the explosive, the speed at which it burns,

259
00:19:10,680 --> 00:19:17,660
and the height of the bed or the quantity of explosive which is evenly spread on the top plate.

260
00:19:17,661 --> 00:19:25,980
The explosive powder is a proprietary blend of common and unique explosive chemicals.

261
00:19:25,981 --> 00:19:34,960
The amount and exact formulation is always matched to the types of metal involved.

262
00:19:34,961 --> 00:19:41,140
Once the pack is set, everyone evacuates the chamber except the blaster in charge, who

263
00:19:41,141 --> 00:19:44,020
remains to wire the detonator.

264
00:19:44,021 --> 00:19:47,600
He will be the last to leave the chamber.

265
00:19:47,601 --> 00:19:55,000
After all personnel are accounted for, the blaster connects his initiator switch to the detonator wires and fires the explosion.

266
00:19:56,260 --> 00:19:57,080
Fire no!

267
00:19:59,000 --> 00:20:05,920
The explosion is detonated from one edge of the clatter and moves across the upper level of the pack at a uniform speed.

268
00:20:07,580 --> 00:20:15,820
This explosive front progressively drives the clatter plate downward toward the backer at the slight collision angle caused by the standoff gap.

269
00:20:17,600 --> 00:20:21,760
Forward of the collision point, air is forced out of the gap at high velocity.

270
00:20:23,080 --> 00:20:29,700
All oxides and impurities are expelled, rendering the plate surfaces metallurgically pure and ideal for a weld.

271
00:20:30,820 --> 00:20:37,100
As the backer and clatter collide, the weld is created nearly instantaneously across the entire surface of the plate.

272
00:20:37,101 --> 00:20:46,880
because of the intense dust created by the explosion workers can't retrieve the newly

273
00:20:46,881 --> 00:20:54,280
formed clad from the explosion chamber for more than 18 hours not surprisingly the power of the

274
00:20:54,281 --> 00:21:01,640
explosion can cause significant deformation to the newly formed clad therefore upon its return

275
00:21:01,641 --> 00:21:03,800
to DMC's central processing facility,

276
00:21:04,620 --> 00:21:07,100
the clad undergoes a final series of corrections.

277
00:21:12,980 --> 00:21:15,500
These include heating the clad in an oven

278
00:21:15,501 --> 00:21:17,700
that causes the metals to soften slightly.

279
00:21:18,180 --> 00:21:20,400
This relieves stress from the blunt force

280
00:21:20,401 --> 00:21:21,580
of the explosion's impact.

281
00:21:23,300 --> 00:21:26,940
Any bowing or misshapen curves are flattened out

282
00:21:26,941 --> 00:21:29,220
by either a 3 million pound press,

283
00:21:29,221 --> 00:21:34,600
or, for thinner clad, by a series of rollers known as levelers.

284
00:21:36,860 --> 00:21:39,440
Finally, before the material is shipped,

285
00:21:40,080 --> 00:21:44,000
stringent testing is conducted to ensure a solid weld between the two plates.

286
00:21:45,440 --> 00:21:51,660
There's a lot of testing because generally these metals will go into a very high-pressure vessel.

287
00:21:51,661 --> 00:21:54,880
Their stakes are extremely high if there's a failure.

288
00:21:54,881 --> 00:22:06,200
So the owners of big plants and chemical manufacturing and oil refineries are extremely concerned that their materials are what they ordered and specified.

289
00:22:06,580 --> 00:22:08,500
There's really no room for error.

290
00:22:09,280 --> 00:22:14,920
Once the material has been proven to meet exacting specs, it's ready to be shipped to the customer.

291
00:22:14,921 --> 00:22:24,520
But explosion welding isn't the only adventurous business, and may look downright tame compared

292
00:22:24,521 --> 00:22:30,800
to welding 325 feet below the ocean surface.

293
00:22:30,801 --> 00:22:36,460
In 1962, DuPont patented the explosion welding process, which it used to produce the clad

294
00:22:36,461 --> 00:22:39,860
metals needed by the U.S. Mint for new coins.

295
00:22:39,861 --> 00:22:45,740
In the next three years, DuPont made over 70 million pounds of dime, quarter, and half-dollar blanks.

296
00:22:46,060 --> 00:22:48,340
Welding will return on Modern Marvels.

297
00:22:51,400 --> 00:22:54,580
We now return to welding on Modern Marvels.

298
00:22:56,920 --> 00:22:59,560
It's a rule that defines common sense.

299
00:23:00,080 --> 00:23:02,960
Don't mix water with electricity. It can kill you.

300
00:23:04,180 --> 00:23:08,000
But there is a rare breed of experts who dare to swim against convention

301
00:23:08,001 --> 00:23:11,380
and perform a job that most consider downright crazy.

302
00:23:15,320 --> 00:23:18,840
Underwater diver welders thrive on the dangers of the deep,

303
00:23:19,360 --> 00:23:22,920
where visibility can be zero and deadly hazards the norm.

304
00:23:24,300 --> 00:23:27,000
Why? To perform the perfect weld.

305
00:23:28,020 --> 00:23:29,820
You're underwater and playing with electricity.

306
00:23:30,260 --> 00:23:33,740
It doesn't seem like a good combination, but it works quite well.

307
00:23:33,741 --> 00:23:41,800
Over the last 30 years, the ocean depths have become vast job sites for the energy and communication industries.

308
00:23:42,420 --> 00:23:51,660
This encompasses everything from pipelines to platforms to subsea wells to inspection, repair and maintenance.

309
00:23:51,980 --> 00:23:55,020
We're working in water depths up to 10,000 feet.

310
00:23:56,520 --> 00:24:01,220
Global Industries is a leader in offshore construction, engineering and support.

311
00:24:01,221 --> 00:24:04,360
for the oil and gas industries around the world.

312
00:24:05,680 --> 00:24:07,560
And to meet this constant demand,

313
00:24:08,160 --> 00:24:10,220
Global Industries requires their welding teams

314
00:24:10,221 --> 00:24:12,360
to undergo years of specialized training

315
00:24:12,361 --> 00:24:14,760
for all types of offshore environments.

316
00:24:18,480 --> 00:24:22,380
We're here at our research and development training center

317
00:24:22,381 --> 00:24:23,740
here in New Iberia, Louisiana.

318
00:24:24,700 --> 00:24:26,640
We're dressing out our diver now,

319
00:24:26,700 --> 00:24:29,500
getting ready to get into the tank and do some training,

320
00:24:29,620 --> 00:24:30,320
do some welding.

321
00:24:31,220 --> 00:24:37,600
There are two basic forms of underwater welding, wet welding and dry welding.

322
00:24:39,220 --> 00:24:47,820
Wet welds are the most common and can be shielded metal arc or stick welds, made by a fully submerged diver welder in a wetsuit.

323
00:24:49,300 --> 00:24:55,220
The electrode is waterproof and inserted into an electrically charged rubber encased stinger.

324
00:24:55,221 --> 00:25:04,360
Once struck, the electric arc burns just as if it were on dry land, since water is equally conductive

325
00:25:04,361 --> 00:25:10,540
Likewise, the electrode's unique waterproof coating shields the weld from water contamination

326
00:25:10,541 --> 00:25:19,760
But in case you're wondering, his rubber gloves are the only things that stand between the diver welder and electric shock

327
00:25:19,761 --> 00:25:29,400
If you have a hole in that glove, then you grab a hold of that live stinger, and that will give you a real good one, and you'll taste your fillings in your mouth.

328
00:25:31,380 --> 00:25:40,883
Fortunately, the odds of a fatal shock are slim, because the welding equipment operates on DC current, which is far less dangerous than the ordinary AC current found

329
00:25:40,884 --> 00:25:41,920
in a typical home.

330
00:25:43,520 --> 00:25:44,860
Calm, shake. Calm, shake.

331
00:25:44,861 --> 00:25:52,780
To get the diver welder fully suited, two other diver welders act as his tenders, checking

332
00:25:52,781 --> 00:25:56,880
and testing every piece of equipment, including the helmet.

333
00:25:56,881 --> 00:26:01,400
Not only does the helmet provide air for the diver welder to breathe, it also serves as

334
00:26:01,401 --> 00:26:07,460
a full welding mask, protecting his eyes from the intense arc light.

335
00:26:07,461 --> 00:26:12,020
Right here we have the lens plate, the face lens that a diver will flip down when he's

336
00:26:12,021 --> 00:26:13,420
ready to start welding.

337
00:26:13,421 --> 00:26:17,020
He'll flip it up when he's finished welding and take a look and see what he's doing.

338
00:26:18,700 --> 00:26:24,260
After the diver welder is suited up, he carefully enters the water and makes his way to the bottom of the tank.

339
00:26:26,660 --> 00:26:34,780
From the moment he puts on his helmet, the diver welder is in constant communication with the control room via audio links and a video feed.

340
00:26:35,400 --> 00:26:37,420
Com check, one, two, three. How do you get me, John?

341
00:26:38,380 --> 00:26:40,100
One by six, lad and clear.

342
00:26:40,360 --> 00:26:41,580
All right, set up the weld.

343
00:26:41,581 --> 00:26:50,500
The trainee must not only learn to strike and maintain an arc, but he must also be able

344
00:26:50,501 --> 00:26:56,480
to connect and reset all of his equipment, including the electrode.

345
00:26:56,481 --> 00:27:01,520
Once in the water, the electrode is inserted into the stinger, which is termed cold, because

346
00:27:01,521 --> 00:27:05,820
the control room has not yet turned on the electricity.

347
00:27:05,821 --> 00:27:09,580
When the diver is ready to weld, he requests that the current be turned on.

348
00:27:09,581 --> 00:27:11,580
Roger, make it hot.

349
00:27:11,581 --> 00:27:13,580
Roger, making it hot.

350
00:27:15,580 --> 00:27:21,580
Like every surface welder, the diver welder must be a master of concentration and stability.

351
00:27:23,580 --> 00:27:28,580
You're going to have a lot of things that can interfere with what you're doing when you're welding.

352
00:27:28,581 --> 00:27:31,580
Just the surge of the ocean can push you around.

353
00:27:31,581 --> 00:27:38,580
Some divers like to actually put their hands up, hold the rod itself, because it gives you a lot of feel on it.

354
00:27:38,581 --> 00:27:43,580
If you just have the rod in your back way back here from where you're working,

355
00:27:43,920 --> 00:27:46,120
you don't have the feel that you need.

356
00:27:48,000 --> 00:27:52,240
Even fish can become distracting and potentially dangerous obstacles.

357
00:27:53,220 --> 00:27:54,360
Barracuda like to watch.

358
00:27:54,940 --> 00:27:55,859
It's a giant fish.

359
00:27:55,860 --> 00:27:59,280
It looks like a torpedo with nasty teeth that stick out everywhere.

360
00:27:59,560 --> 00:28:04,200
And they like just to kind of hover over your shoulder to see what you're doing all the time.

361
00:28:04,201 --> 00:28:09,740
And you're just kind of like, what's, ah, but they're just doing their own thing.

362
00:28:11,520 --> 00:28:19,420
But sometimes a job requires a diver welder to go to extreme depths and stay in that environment for days, even weeks.

363
00:28:20,560 --> 00:28:24,880
This is called saturation diving and requires serious courage.

364
00:28:26,320 --> 00:28:31,920
It may also require the highly sophisticated technologies of dry underwater welding.

365
00:28:34,200 --> 00:28:41,660
The difference between wet and dry underwater welding, the dry underwater weld will require a habitat.

366
00:28:42,940 --> 00:28:49,260
A habitat is lowered down and put in place on the member or on the pipeline that's going to be repaired.

367
00:28:49,900 --> 00:28:56,820
Once it's installed, they seal it and then they actually put the air down to it and pump all the water out of it.

368
00:28:57,300 --> 00:29:00,980
This allows that piece to be basically dry now.

369
00:29:00,981 --> 00:29:13,221
And so the divers can go down and they'll actually climb up inside the habitat, remove their helmets, and actually be able to go to work and weld that particular item

370
00:29:13,222 --> 00:29:14,180
in the drive.

371
00:29:17,200 --> 00:29:22,100
Dry underwater diver welders work in pairs and in six to eight hour shifts.

372
00:29:22,101 --> 00:29:28,640
when done they return to the surface in a diving bell

373
00:29:28,641 --> 00:29:32,220
then enter a second habitat maintained aboard ship

374
00:29:32,221 --> 00:29:39,180
within these on-ship habitats they will eat sleep and relax before returning to the ocean floor

375
00:29:39,181 --> 00:29:45,380
both the diving bell and on-ship habitat maintain the same pressure

376
00:29:45,381 --> 00:29:48,800
as if the divers were still a thousand feet underwater

377
00:29:48,801 --> 00:29:55,580
This is the only efficient and practical way for their bodies to remain adjusted to such extreme pressures

378
00:29:55,581 --> 00:30:03,300
Otherwise the diver welders may experience the bends and other physical perils even death

379
00:30:03,301 --> 00:30:11,380
To prepare diver welders for these conditions global industries has built an unparalleled underwater simulator

380
00:30:12,280 --> 00:30:14,560
This facility that you see behind me right here

381
00:30:14,561 --> 00:30:18,220
It's really the only one of its kind in the United States.

382
00:30:18,221 --> 00:30:19,640
We call this the dry pot.

383
00:30:20,440 --> 00:30:24,620
We can carry out dry welding scenarios down to a thousand feet.

384
00:30:25,700 --> 00:30:29,640
A hyperbaric chamber simulates the high pressures the human body must endure

385
00:30:29,641 --> 00:30:31,780
when working at great ocean depths.

386
00:30:33,840 --> 00:30:35,380
Akin to being an astronaut,

387
00:30:35,980 --> 00:30:39,600
the trainee will spend more than a week living in this simulated environment.

388
00:30:40,200 --> 00:30:42,020
And conditions are tight.

389
00:30:42,021 --> 00:30:46,220
It's very, very cramped inside there.

390
00:30:46,520 --> 00:30:48,580
There's a place for everything to go,

391
00:30:48,800 --> 00:30:51,880
and you have to be sure that you put everything in its place

392
00:30:51,881 --> 00:30:54,300
before you go and start doing things,

393
00:30:54,380 --> 00:30:57,540
because there are a lot of things that can bite you in there.

394
00:31:00,220 --> 00:31:01,620
Unlike the wet welder,

395
00:31:01,980 --> 00:31:05,880
the dry welder can perform more than stick welds in his cramped habitat.

396
00:31:06,720 --> 00:31:10,120
I'm standing right in front of the entrance to the dry pot hatch.

397
00:31:10,121 --> 00:31:13,060
Royce is doing gas tungsten arc welding.

398
00:31:13,061 --> 00:31:17,420
It's also known as TIG welding, which is an old word for tungsten inner gas.

399
00:31:18,880 --> 00:31:24,100
Once his training is finished, the trainee enters this decompression chamber

400
00:31:24,101 --> 00:31:27,820
to slowly acclimate his body back to the normal atmosphere.

401
00:31:29,800 --> 00:31:33,540
But while even these adrenaline junkies need to rest once in a while,

402
00:31:34,280 --> 00:31:35,960
some welders never take a break.

403
00:31:36,460 --> 00:31:38,060
Of course, they're robots.

404
00:31:40,120 --> 00:31:40,820
The U.S.

405
00:31:40,821 --> 00:31:47,400
Navy recently set a world record by completing a wet weld at 2,000 feet below the surface.

406
00:31:48,400 --> 00:31:51,920
The job? To train for deep sea submarine rescue.

407
00:31:53,540 --> 00:31:56,040
Welding will return on Modern Marvels.

408
00:31:59,140 --> 00:32:02,520
We now return to welding on Modern Marvels.

409
00:32:05,600 --> 00:32:08,780
Welding. The heart and soul of making a car.

410
00:32:10,120 --> 00:32:13,000
And for major automobile manufacturers like General Motors,

411
00:32:14,860 --> 00:32:18,120
no process is more vital or more automated.

412
00:32:19,940 --> 00:32:22,000
When I first started with General Motors,

413
00:32:22,380 --> 00:32:25,780
there weren't any robots in the body shop I worked in.

414
00:32:25,940 --> 00:32:27,800
Two years later, there were 40 of them.

415
00:32:27,980 --> 00:32:29,780
In the body shop we're standing in now,

416
00:32:29,880 --> 00:32:32,100
there's approximately 1,200 robots.

417
00:32:32,820 --> 00:32:36,240
Over a third of them, 480-some, are welding robots.

418
00:32:36,241 --> 00:32:45,240
Here at GM's Lake Orion assembly plant, more than 16,000 cars roll off the lines every month.

419
00:32:45,241 --> 00:32:51,240
By the time each car is fully assembled, it will contain thousands of welds.

420
00:32:51,241 --> 00:32:58,240
On this structure as it sits right here, there's approximately 3,800 welds.

421
00:32:58,241 --> 00:33:02,240
Once the outer structure goes on, there'll be approximately 1,200 more welds added to it

422
00:33:02,241 --> 00:33:05,240
for approximately 5,000 welds overall on the vehicle.

423
00:33:06,240 --> 00:33:17,560
Nearly all those welds are completed by robotic welding systems, which load, position and

424
00:33:17,561 --> 00:33:21,420
weld more than 240 component parts.

425
00:33:21,421 --> 00:33:27,800
Most of the robots that are used for welding are actually used for spot welding, and probably

426
00:33:27,801 --> 00:33:33,760
60% of the welding robots that are out there are actually spot welding robots.

427
00:33:33,761 --> 00:33:38,580
Unlike arc welding, spot welding does not create a bead, nor is it designed for heavy

428
00:33:38,581 --> 00:33:41,000
steel items like beams.

429
00:33:41,001 --> 00:33:48,560
Instead, spot welds bond thin metal sheets, as in car doors, or hoods.

430
00:33:48,561 --> 00:33:52,920
Energy is focused to a single spot, where two electrodes make direct contact with the

431
00:33:52,921 --> 00:33:55,240
metal sheets to be joined.

432
00:33:55,241 --> 00:33:59,560
The electrodes are made of copper because it has low electrical resistance and high

433
00:33:59,561 --> 00:34:01,440
thermal conductivity.

434
00:34:01,441 --> 00:34:06,240
This means it can deliver some serious juice.

435
00:34:06,241 --> 00:34:11,400
This particular unit is a pneumatic operated spot welding system that's got a 75 kVA transformer

436
00:34:11,401 --> 00:34:13,500
back here.

437
00:34:13,501 --> 00:34:16,240
The electrodes up here are all water cooled.

438
00:34:16,241 --> 00:34:20,200
This particular set up here probably runs about 10 to 12,000 amps through these electrodes

439
00:34:20,201 --> 00:34:21,200
right here.

440
00:34:21,201 --> 00:34:26,420
It's not uncommon to see 40,000, 50,000 amps run through the electrodes.

441
00:34:26,421 --> 00:34:31,700
Given that most houses run on less than 200 amps, that's a lot of power.

442
00:34:31,701 --> 00:34:34,980
But it's not the only factor in a reliable spot weld.

443
00:34:34,981 --> 00:34:37,520
You need pressure.

444
00:34:37,521 --> 00:34:42,680
When the two electrodes come together, they pinch the two metal sheets at the spot and

445
00:34:42,681 --> 00:34:47,940
cause a small indentation in both.

446
00:34:47,941 --> 00:34:52,020
Electric current then begins to pass from one electrode to the other through this spot

447
00:34:52,021 --> 00:34:54,460
in the sheet metal.

448
00:34:54,461 --> 00:34:59,340
As it does so, the current that flowed so smoothly through the copper now encounters

449
00:34:59,341 --> 00:35:03,120
resistance in the less conductive metal pieces.

450
00:35:03,121 --> 00:35:08,080
This resistance results in heat, and the metal begins to melt, causing a molten nugget to

451
00:35:08,081 --> 00:35:09,080
form.

452
00:35:09,081 --> 00:35:15,980
When the molten nugget cools and coalesces, it locks the two metal sheets together.

453
00:35:15,981 --> 00:35:19,580
The electrodes then release the pinch point and move on.

454
00:35:19,581 --> 00:35:24,020
A robot can make a series of spot welds in a relatively short period of time.

455
00:35:24,021 --> 00:35:26,020
For a man, it's a heavier task.

456
00:35:26,021 --> 00:35:32,020
A typical spot welding gun might weigh anywhere from 100 pounds to 200 pounds.

457
00:35:32,021 --> 00:35:35,900
That's a lot of physical labor to move a spot welding gun.

458
00:35:35,901 --> 00:35:40,700
And so a robot that's designed to handle that kind of weight is an ideal setup, because

459
00:35:40,701 --> 00:35:45,640
the robot can just handle the spot welding gun and consistently put it in the same place

460
00:35:45,641 --> 00:35:47,640
every time.

